+# Search pantry items in Postgres
+
+For whoever changes item search or proposes a search service. Pantry item search runs on Postgres full-text search,
+not Elasticsearch, because a third service in a year would need a volunteer to run it, and Postgres already does the
+job.
+
+## Context
+
+In October 2025 a volunteer reported that searching the pantry for "rice" took four seconds and missed "Rice,
+brown"; another volunteer agreed it was painful on the phone. Elasticsearch was proposed to fix both, with a
+volunteer ready to set it up that weekend.
+
+An ops volunteer raised the operating question before adding it: who runs the service when that volunteer is away.
+Larder had been here twice. In 2023, Redis proposed as a session store was turned down because only one volunteer
+would have run it ([keep sessions in the database](0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md)). In March 2025, Redis
+holding the Celery job queue evicted 300 receipt jobs one Saturday night, and the one volunteer who could restore it
+spent a day resending them by hand ([run background jobs on Postgres](0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md)). Both
+times Postgres, the database larder already runs, did the job instead. This was the third time in the year the same
+argument for a new service came up, which is why it is now written down as a tenet: [larder runs one database and one
+web app](../philosophy.md#larder-runs-one-database-and-one-web-app). Two forces judge the options: search must be
+fast and find near-matches such as "Rice, brown" for a search on "rice", and the volunteers keep the number of
+services they operate down.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Elasticsearch**: fast relevance ranking and fuzzy matching; lost because it is a new service to run, and nobody
+  could name a second volunteer able to restore it, the same objection that turned down Redis twice before.
+- **Postgres full-text search**: chosen.
+
+## Decision
+
+Pantry item search uses Postgres full-text search on the item name, because it brings a search for "rice" down to
+40 ms and finds "Rice, brown", without adding a service to run.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Search stays inside the database already backed up nightly, with no new service to operate. Matching is limited to
+what Postgres's text-search dictionaries cover, which may need tuning as the pantry's item names grow.
